    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\nTesla Expected To Launch IFTTT-Style Automations in North America and Europe With the Holiday Update\n12 photos\nPhoto: Maciej Wilczy?ski and Micha? Gapi?ski via X.com\nIn January, Tesla introduced automations in China, allowing owners to set \"If This, Then That\" (IFTTT) routines. Now, the carmaker is ready to expand the feature in Western markets, most probably with this winter's Holiday Update. The software already has the necessary changes for the automations to run, as revealed in recent social media posts.\nAnyone with a smart home is familiar with automations, although the feature has also been popular among smartphone users for a long time. Apps like IFTTT (short for If This, Then That) have been used for years to automate tasks, and Apple has offered a Shortcuts app doing pretty much the same thing.\nIt was only a matter of time before Tesla would provide a similar functionality. As with many other new functions, this was first offered to customers in China with the 2025.45 32.1 update . Much like Apple, Tesla offered this via a dedicated app, aptly called \"Automation.\" At the time, I speculated that the feature would also be offered in Western markets soon.\nAs predicted, Tesla is now preparing to roll out the Automation app outside China. The feature has been discovered in the Tesla software, and it appears to be a translation of the Chinese app. The discovery has been made by Maciej Wilczyński and Michał Gapiński, two Polish developers working on a joint project related to Tesla. Gapiński is best known to the Tesla community for the Tesla Android Project.\nWilczyński appreciated the Automation app as \"fully polished,\" albeit still hidden under a feature flag, waiting to be unlocked. The developers were able to do this on their personal Teslas, and they shared videos showing how it works. The rest of Tesla owners will likely get the app with the upcoming Holiday Update , sometime in December.\nThe Automation app uses a typical \"If This, Then That\" logic, which should be familiar to anyone who used the Apple Shortcuts app on their iPhone or Tasker on an Android phone. Wilczyński's video shows how simple it is to create an automation. You just need to choose an action, a trigger, and a condition (or more) from a list of available options.\nGapiński, for instance, used the Automation app to remind his wife to use FSD (now renamed Tesla Assisted Driving in Europe) every time she fastens her seatbelt, but there really are countless possibilities. For instance, owners could set location-based reminders, time-of-day automations, and other alerts and actions based on the driver's presence or vehicle state.\nEventually, people will be able to design their own automations, adopt the ones designed by others, or choose from a marketplace. There are some limits to what the app can do, and the most important is that the screen must be active. This excludes automations running in the background while the car is parked with no one inside. Apparently, automation can't be triggered when Tesla's driver assistance features (Autopilot or TAD/ FSD ) are active, either. Just to put more context into how the automations app works with our little @CoilShift setup. Our @maciej forgot to show what it can do My wife will now get a reminder to use FSD every time her seatbelts gets fastened. Neat! It’s like the first version of Shortcuts on iOS ages ago. @TeslaNewswire @NotATeslaApp — Michał Gapiński (@mikegapinski) October 12, 2026 Back in January @NotATeslaApp reported that Tesla is set to introduce an Automations app in the car infotainment system. It seems that it’s now fully polished and present in latest firmware revisions, but hidden behind a feature flag. Thanks to @CoilShift I’m now able to present it to you in its full glory. The app is absolutely stock, I didn’t make any modifications inside. — Maciej P. Wilczyński (@maciej) October 12, 2026\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\ntesla automation Tesla automations ifttt apple shortcuts tesla software holiday update\nAbout the author: Cristian Agatie\nAfter his childhood dream of becoming a \"tractor operator\" didn't pan out, Cristian turned to journalism, first in print and later moving to online media. His top interests are electric vehicles and new energy solutions. Full profile\nUP NEXT Canadians Lean European on New Cars, Many Warm Up to Electric Vehicles",
